Hardware CLUT expansion on the PPA: 8-bit indexed -> RGB565, in the blend
engine, with no CPU touching the pixels.

WHY THESE ARE WHOLE FILES AND NOT A DIFF
----------------------------------------
linux-71-port/ is not a git repository and there is no pristine copy of it in
the tree, so `git diff` produces nothing and there is no base to diff against.
Storing the four modified files whole is recoverable; a patch that cannot be
generated is not. Copy them back over:

    linux-71-port/drivers/gpu/drm/espressif/esp32s31-ppa.c
    linux-71-port/drivers/gpu/drm/espressif/esp32s31-ppa.h
    linux-71-port/drivers/gpu/drm/espressif/esp32s31-lcd.c
    linux-71-port/include/uapi/drm/esp32s31_drm.h

WHAT IS IN THEM
---------------
* PPA CLUT registers, which Espressif's S31 documentation does not mention and
  their driver leaves as "TODO: Support CLUT to support L4/L8 color mode":
      0x000 BLEND0_CLUT_DATA (ARGB8888 FIFO port)   0x004 BLEND1_CLUT_DATA
      0x00c CLUT_CONF: bit 0 apb_fifo_mask (0 = FIFO), 1 blend0_mem_rst,
                       3 blend0_rdaddr_rst, 6 blend0_force_pu, 7 blend0_clk_ena
  Verified on silicon before any of this was written: 256 entries pushed
  through the FIFO and read back byte-exact.
* Pixel-byte encodings from IDF's dma2d_types.h. They are NOT bytes-1:
      0 = 0.5 B/px (L4)   1 = 1 B/px (L8)   3 = 2 B/px (RGB565)   5 = 4 B/px
* esp32s31_ppa_clut_expand(): loads the CLUT, sets blend0_rx_cm = L8 (4) and
  blend_tx_cm = RGB565 (2), points the foreground at the destination with a
  fixed alpha of ZERO so it contributes nothing, and runs the blend. It is a
  colour conversion wearing a blend's clothes, because that is the only shape
  the hardware offers for an indexed input.
* DRM_IOCTL_ESP32S31_PPA_CLUT, ioctl number 0x05.

TWO TRAPS THAT COST TIME HERE, SO THEY ARE NOT REPEATED
-------------------------------------------------------
1. `make linux` builds linux-71-port, NOT the linux-esp32-s31 submodule. The
   first version of this went into the submodule and silently was not built -
   the symbol was simply absent from System.map. See memory/s31-wrong-kernel-tree.
2. DRM_IOCTL_DEF_DRV uses a DESIGNATED INITIALISER indexed by the ioctl
   number, so the table's source order is irrelevant and a duplicate number
   silently loses. PPA_CLUT was first given 0x01, which is JPEG_REC; the
   later entry won the slot, the new handler was never installed, and the call
   landed in the JPEG code and returned EINVAL. Numbers in use: 0x00 PPA_COPY,
   0x01 JPEG_REC, 0x02 JPEG_FRAME, 0x03 PPA_BLEND, 0x04 JPEG_THUMB,
   0x05 PPA_CLUT.

PROVEN
------
rootfs/cluttest.c allocates two GEM dumb buffers (the blend engine can only
address that reserved region - a client's ordinary pages are invisible to it),
fills one with every index, and checks the result against the palette:

    CT PASS: 0 of 64000 pixels wrong


RED-TEAMED AND WIDENED, 2026-09-07
----------------------------------
The files above were applied unreviewed once, which is not good enough for
something DRM_RENDER_ALLOW reachable. Reviewing them found five defects and
one design limitation; all are fixed here.

1. INTEGER OVERFLOW, userspace-triggerable. `(size_t)w * h` is 32-bit here, so
   w = h = 0x10000 wraps the product to 0, passes every size check - including
   esp32s31_ppa_in_range() - and is then shifted into the PPA_BLEND_HB/VB
   bitfields where it corrupts the neighbours. Both dimensions are now bounded
   by ESP32S31_PPA_CLUT_MAX_DIM (4096) before any arithmetic.

2. UP TO ~400 ms OF STALL with ppa->lock held. The wait was
   spin_rx_done, then wait_for_completion_timeout(200ms), then ANOTHER 200 ms
   cpu_relax loop - on a single-core board, blocking the cursor plane and the
   thumbnail path behind it. The expansion measures ~2.5 ms, so past a few
   tens of ms it has failed and spinning only converts a dropped frame into a
   freeze. Now one 50 ms wait plus a single re-read of the raw EOF bit (which
   can legitimately race the completion). NOTE: the identical 200+200 ms wait
   is STILL PRESENT in the copy and blend paths in esp32s31-ppa.c. Left alone
   deliberately - they are the shipping cursor and thumbnail paths and this was
   changed while measuring something else. Worth its own before/after.

3. THE PALETTE WAS RE-UPLOADED EVERY CALL - 256 FIFO register writes per
   frame for something that changes on a level load if at all. Cached now.

4. COLD-START BUG IN THAT CACHE, found by review rather than by test:
   clut_cache is zero-initialised and the hardware FIFO is undefined at boot,
   so a first caller passing an all-zero palette would compare equal, skip the
   upload and expand through whatever was in the FIFO. A clut_valid flag now
   forces the first load. cluttest would never have caught it.

5. THE CACHE SYNC DESTROYED THE REST OF THE FRAMEBUFFER. Syncing the whole
   destination DMA_FROM_DEVICE invalidates that range, discarding anything the
   CPU wrote and had not flushed - for a compositor, the chrome it just drew
   around the window. rootfs/cluttest2 caught it: block pixel-perfect, 28,434
   pixels around it zeroed. Now synced per row, over only the rows the device
   writes.

DESIGN: THE IOCTL COULD NOT TARGET A SUB-RECTANGLE. It expanded into a tight
w*h buffer at the origin, which forces a compositor to blit that buffer into
the framebuffer afterwards - the very copy the hardware path exists to avoid,
measured at 40% of the compositor's per-frame cost. The 2D-DMA descriptor has
always taken a block at (x,y) within a larger picture; only the struct could
not say so. dst_x/dst_y/dst_pic_w/dst_pic_h are APPENDED, so DRM zero-fills
them for callers built against the old struct and zero means "tight, at the
origin" - the previous behaviour exactly.

PROVEN, on silicon, all three at once:

    cluttest   (old struct)      PASS 0 of 64000 pixels wrong
    cluttest   (again, cached)   PASS 0 of 64000 pixels wrong
    cluttest2  (block at 151,97 in 800x480, an ODD offset on purpose)
                                 PASS 0 wrong in block, 0 spilled outside

The spill check is the half that matters for a compositor: writing outside the
window corrupts other windows, and that damage reads as a rendering bug
somewhere else entirely.
